Let me explain fetching calmly, the way I would to a junior joining our trading desk team. When Hibernate loads a parent entity, the big question is: should it also load the related entities right now, or wait until someone actually asks for them? That decision is the fetch type.

`EAGER` loading means the related entities are loaded IMMEDIATELY, together with the parent query. Hibernate does this using either a SQL JOIN or a separate subselect fired at the same time. So the moment you load a `Trade`, its `Account` is already sitting in memory, fully populated. `LAZY` loading is the opposite — the related entity or collection is loaded ONLY when you first touch it. Hibernate hands you a proxy object (a lightweight stand-in), and the real SQL only fires the instant you call a method on that proxy. For example, `trade.getAccount().getHolderName()` is what actually triggers the `SELECT` against the accounts table.

The defaults catch almost everyone out, so memorise them. `@ManyToOne` and `@OneToOne` default to EAGER. `@OneToMany` and `@ManyToMany` default to LAZY. The dangerous one is the `@ManyToOne` EAGER default. Imagine we list two hundred trades — every single `Trade` eagerly drags its `Account` along, even on a screen that only shows the trade symbol and amount. That is a classic source of the N+1 problem. The fix is to always override it:

```java
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
private Account account;
```

`LazyInitializationException` is the error juniors fear the most, so let me demystify it. It happens when you access a LAZY association AFTER the Hibernate Session (the transaction) has already closed. The proxy wakes up, tries to fire its SQL to load the data, but there is no open session left to run that query — so it throws. This almost always shows up in REST controllers that serialize an entity to JSON outside the `@Transactional` boundary. Here is the trap:

```java
// Service returns a managed entity, TX closes when method returns
Trade trade = tradeRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
// Later, in the controller during JSON serialization:
trade.getAccount().getHolderName();  // proxy fires SQL — but session is CLOSED → LazyInitializationException
```

The correct fix is to load what you need WHILE the transaction is still open — using a JOIN FETCH query, an `@EntityGraph`, or best of all a DTO projection. You should NOT fix it by re-enabling open-in-view, because that just hides the problem and keeps the database connection pinned for the whole request.

```java
// Fix with JOIN FETCH — Account loaded inside the transaction, in one query
@Query("SELECT t FROM Trade t JOIN FETCH t.account WHERE t.account.id = :accountId")
List<Trade> findWithAccount(@Param("accountId") String accountId);
```

`Hibernate.initialize(entity.getTrades())` is a tool for forcing a LAZY association to load while the session is still open. For example, inside a service method you can call `Hibernate.initialize(account.getTrades())` and the collection is populated before the transaction ends. But be honest about its limit — it fires an ADDITIONAL query, so if you call it in a loop over many accounts you have simply recreated N+1. It does not solve the performance problem; it only avoids the exception.

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public void warmUp(Account account) {
    Hibernate.initialize(account.getTrades());  // one extra SELECT, session still open
}
```

`getReference(id)` returns a LAZY proxy WITHOUT hitting the database at all. It is perfect when you only need the foreign key, not the full row. For example, when creating a trade you just need the `account_id` column set — you do not need Rajesh's name or balance. So `trade.setAccount(em.getReference(Account.class, accountId))` lets Hibernate write the FK without ever loading the Account:

```java
Account ref = em.getReference(Account.class, "SAV2001");  // no SELECT — just a proxy with the id
Trade trade = new Trade("TRD-001", "RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("245050"));
trade.setAccount(ref);
em.persist(trade);  // INSERT trades (..., account_id='SAV2001') — Account never loaded
```

`findById(id)` is the opposite: it hits the database immediately and returns an `Optional`. Use it when you genuinely need the entity's data right now, for example to read and modify the balance. A senior-level nuance: `getReference` will only throw `EntityNotFoundException` LATER (when the proxy is first touched), whereas `findById` lets you check `isPresent()` straight away. So reach for `getReference` for FK-only writes, and `findById` when you need to read the actual state.

